title: Against Mid-Acts Dispensationalism (Acts 15:8-11)
description: Peter held the same gospel of grace as Paul; all men from Adam through today have been saved by the grace of God through faith.
output: lessons/heresies/against-mid-acts.html

# From studies/data/notes/against-mid-acts-notes.html, in the author's words, arranged into slides.

=== Acts 15
--- Against Mid-Acts Dispensationalism [title]
overline: Answering error
heading: Against Mid-Acts Dispensationalism
sub: Acts 15:8-11
hint: Use the arrow keys or swipe to move through the slides. Tap any gold reference to read the verse.

--- Even as they
scripture: Acts 15:8-11 | mark: But we believe that through the grace of the LORD Jesus Christ we shall be saved, even as they

--- What "Mid-Acts" holds
panel-warn: "Mid-Acts" Dispensationalism | "Mid-Acts" Dispensationalism holds that by chapter 15, a different gospel is made known unto Gentiles, that the Jews keeping the law of Moses still, despite believing, were under a dispensation of the law still. That the Gospel of Grace was given to Paul to preach to the Gentiles. That these verses "prove it."

--- A plain reading
lead: A plain reading of the text shows that Peter held the same Gospel of Grace, and that it is not given by works of the law, but by the grace of the {Lord}
p: (made distinct in the text, using the same capitalized distinction reserved for YHWH. A divine Lord, absolute authority, Greek is Kyrios).

=== One gospel
--- The same gospel
big: Acts 15:11
p: This sentence refutes any of the idea that Jews had a different gospel or standard, or that Peter's preaching to the circumcision was any different than Paul's ministry to the Gentiles.

--- There is no "Mid-Acts Dispensation" [title]
heading: There is no "Mid-Acts Dispensation."
lead: All men, from Adam through today have been saved by the grace of God the Father, through faith in His deliverance, which is by His Son, Jesus Christ.
